                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues that venture capital\u0027s structural incentives ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â pricing companies by comparables rather than intrinsic value, and chasing power-law returns ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â systematically bias founders and VCs toward revenue scaling at the expense of profitable business models. He identifies six factors that determine true scalability (market size, growth, industry structure, capital intensity, customer inertia, and key-person dependency) and maps eight outcome scenarios from best-case \u0027Lightning in a Bottle\u0027 to worst-case \u0027Cut Your Losses.\u0027 He contends that changes in capital markets ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â longer private-company lifespans, public equity funding of private firms ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â have removed the historical discipline of IPO scrutiny, producing \u0027big and broken\u0027 businesses with sky-high valuations built on questionable economics.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran is essentially writing the autopsy report for a generation of AI infrastructure companies before they have died. The parallels to the current AI capex supercycle are direct: massive investment, narrative-driven valuations, and the assumption that monetization will follow scale. The real risk he is identifying is not that AI fails technologically ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â it is that even successful AI adoption gets priced so aggressively into valuations that returns to equity investors are poor regardless of the underlying business outcomes. For enterprise leaders evaluating AI vendor stability, this framework is a practical due diligence lens: ask whether your key AI vendors have a credible path to profitable unit economics, not just impressive revenue growth, before building critical dependencies on them.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
